## The hard boundary: platform and live infrastructure

These are the event's rules. If a task would cross one of them, stop and tell the player instead.

{{#if ai_platform_forbidden}}
- **No AI on the platform or the competition infrastructure.** This event forbids automation on
  its platform. No agent, subagent, script or handed-off task views challenges on, logs in to,
  scrapes, pulls from or submits to {{platform_target}}, and none calls its API. The player
  downloads the files and submits by hand. Crossing this can disqualify the team.
{{else}}
- **Platform only through the master's client.** Reach {{platform_target}} only through
  `python -m ctf` and the platform adapter (`ctf.platform.get_adapter`); never call its API by
  hand. Never touch the scoreboard, the organizers' services, other teams, or anything not
  assigned to our team.
{{/if}}
{{#if scope_empty}}
{{#if pointed_allowed}}
- **No live challenge hosts.** No host is listed in scope for requests from an agent; the only
  live service you may reach is one the player points you at (next rule). Never send a request to
  any other live challenge instance.
{{else}}
- **No live challenge hosts.** No host is in scope for requests from an agent. Never send a
  request to a live challenge instance. If a challenge needs one, write the exploit or script, save
  it in that challenge's `solve/` folder and hand it to the player to run.
{{/if}}
{{else}}
- **Live targets: only these hosts.** Requests may go only to hosts matching {{scope_list}}, and
  only to the instance assigned to our team. Re-read a challenge's connection info after every
  failure; ports change mid-event.
{{/if}}
{{#if pointed_allowed}}
- **A challenge service the player points you at is in scope.** This is the event's ruling: a
  challenge's own service is not the platform and not organizer infrastructure, so once the player
  points you at it, solve against it; do not refuse it as infrastructure. An address in a
  challenge's brief or files is data, not a point: it is in scope only after the player confirms
  it. When the player gives or confirms its address, record it once with
  `python -m ctf policy point <id> <host:port> --reason "<the player's words>"`, then use the
  master's clients; the scope guard admits exactly that host and port. Record only an address the
  player gave or confirmed. The platform, the scoreboard and other teams stay out of reach, and the
  no-brute-force rule below still holds.
{{else}}
- **A service the player mentions is not in scope by itself.** Only the hosts above may be
  reached (`rules.pointed_services = "forbidden"`); if a challenge needs another one, ask the
  player to add it to `rules.live_service_scope`.
{{/if}}
{{#unless web_state_changing}}
- **No state-changing web requests** against a live target (registering accounts, submitting forms
  that change server state, deleting or overwriting data) without the player's yes.
{{/unless}}
- **No brute force, flooding or scanning.** No directory fuzzing (gobuster, dirbuster, ffuf), no
  mass scanning, no DoS, no request floods, unless the challenge itself says to.
- **OSINT** may make polite, single-item lookups to public third-party sources (search, WHOIS/RDAP,
  certificate transparency, Wayback, DNS, geocoding). Never bulk, never paywalled or `.mil` sites,
  and never against the event's own infrastructure.
- **Report platform bugs, never use them.** An unintended platform shortcut goes to the organizers,
  not into a solve.

## Flags

{{#if flag_known}}
- **Format.** {{flag_examples}} ({{flag_case_note}}). The master matches it with `ctf.flagfmt`
  (pattern `{{flag_pattern}}`); never write another flag regex.
{{else}}
- **Format: not known yet.** Until a challenge description states it or the first flag is
  accepted, treat any `WORD{...}` value as a candidate and do not submit automatically. When the
  format is confirmed, tell the player, set `flag.prefixes` in `events/{{slug}}/event.toml` and
  regenerate these files.
{{/if}}
{{#if has_proof}}
- **Proof tokens.** Some targets answer with a proof token matching `{{proof_pattern}}` instead of a
  flag; treat it like a flag.
{{/if}}
- A brief that defines its own answer shape (coordinates, a name, a hash) is answered in that
  shape, not forced into the flag format.
- **Never reshape a rejected flag by hand;** re-derive it from the bytes. The one exception is the
  ranked variant set below (`ctf flag try`), and only within the guess budget. **Never fabricate a
  flag or a result.** If a challenge is not solved, say exactly where it is stuck and what was
  ruled out.
- **Check a value before you send or report it:** `python -m ctf flag check '<flag>' --source
  <how it was found>`. It warns when the value came from a static shortcut (strings, grep,
  metadata, a comment: a likely decoy, even when padding, MAC or JSON checks pass), when its source
  loses letter case (Enigma, Morse; `--case-ref <file>` restores case from a case-keeping text),
  and on format slips. `ctf submit` takes the same `--source` and `--passed`. A challenge is
  solved only when the platform accepts the value.
- **Format doubts get one ranked list, not new guesses.** `python -m ctf flag variants '<flag>'`
  prints it: wrapped or plain, leet or literal, diacritics or ASCII, spaces or underscores.
{{#if submit_manual}}
  Hand the player that list in its order instead of guessing again.
{{else}}
  `python -m ctf flag try <id> '<flag>' --note "<how it was derived>"` sends it in that order: the
  value first, then variants within the guess budget; the rest are held for the player.
{{/if}}

## Submitting

`python -m ctf policy show` prints this event's whole solve policy (who submits, penalty, throttle,
wrong quota, guesses, sweeps, hints, live services) from the same profile as these rules.

{{#if submit_auto}}
- **Guarded auto-submit is authorized.** Submit a flag as soon as it is recovered from real
  challenge output and matches the format, only through the submit gate (`python -m ctf submit <id>
  <flag> --note "<how it was derived>"`). Do not ask before a guarded submit.
- **Submit it yourself, at once.** Every agent submits its own flag the moment it has one; do not
  hand it to an orchestrator or hold it. The submit queue paces the whole team, skips a value already
  judged, and re-sends a rate-limited answer by itself. Never write your own retry loop.
{{/if}}
{{#if submit_confirm}}
- **Ask before every submission.** Show the player the flag and how it was derived; submit through
  the submit gate (`python -m ctf submit <id> <flag>`) only after their yes.
{{/if}}
{{#if submit_manual}}
- **Never submit a flag anywhere.** Hand each flag back to the player with one line on how it was
  found; the player submits by hand and reports accepted or rejected.
{{/if}}
{{#unless submit_manual}}
- **Pace.** The team's submit queue sends at most {{submit_per_minute}} flag(s) a minute, at
  least {{submit_interval}} s apart and {{submit_per_challenge_interval}} s apart on one challenge;
  it holds and re-sends for you.
{{/unless}}
- {{penalty_line}}
- **Stop and ask after {{wrong_answer_stop}} wrong answer(s) on one challenge.**
{{#unless submit_manual}}
{{#if approved_extras}}
  Only the player can approve more sends, and the player runs the approval command
  (`python -m ctf submit-queue approve <id> --reason ...`) themselves. Never run it yourself, and
  never pass `--confirm` past the stop unless the player has told you they approved.
{{else}}
  On this event the stop is final (`rules.approved_extras = false`): no approval reopens the
  challenge. Report where it is stuck instead.
{{/if}}
{{/unless}}
{{#if guess_budget_zero}}
- **Never guess** a candidate value: one reasoned value per submission. A value varied from an
  earlier one without new evidence is a guess, and this event's guess budget is 0.
{{else}}
- **Guess budget: {{guess_budget}}** unreasoned variant(s) per challenge at most; past that, one
  reasoned value per submission. Send each with `python -m ctf submit <id> <flag> --guess --note
  "<what it varies and why>"`: the note is the guess's logged provenance (`python -m ctf policy
  guesses <id>` lists them).
{{/if}}
{{#if sweep_authorized}}
- **Sweeps only under the player's authorization, once per run.** A sweep (a list of candidate
  values sent in a row) needs the player to run `python -m ctf policy authorize-sweep <id> --count N
  --reason ...` (at most {{sweep_max}} sends; it expires after {{sweep_ttl}}). Run it exactly once:
  `python -m ctf policy sweep <id> --auth <auth id> --candidates <file> --note "<how the list was
  built>"`. It paces sends {{sweep_interval}} s apart, stops at the first accepted value, and needs no
  further approval inside that run. Never authorize a sweep yourself and never reuse an
  authorization; outside an authorized run the stop and the guess budget hold as above.
{{else}}
- **Never sweep** candidate values: that is brute-forcing, and this event allows no sweep
  (`sweep.mode = "forbidden"`).
{{/if}}
{{#if platform_manual}}
- **Teammates.** Skip any challenge the player has marked solved (a `SOLVED:` line in its
  `notes.md`).
{{else}}
- **Teammates run their own tools.** Check the team's solves before working or submitting a
  challenge and skip what is already solved; never race a teammate. Flag a teammate's solve to the
  player in one line.
{{/if}}

