## Spawning agents

- **One owner per challenge, at most 5 agents on it.** Before you start any helper (a Codex
  `spawn_agent`, a Claude subagent, a launcher run), run `python -m ctf spawn request <id> --lane
  <lane> --task "<one line>"`. Exit 0: start the helper as it says. Exit 3 (held): the challenge has
  its 5 agents; do not start one. More agents are the player's decision (`python -m ctf spawn allow`);
  never run it yourself.
- **Brief, not fork.** Start the helper from the brief file the request prints, with `fork_turns:
  "none"`; the brief carries the challenge, the evidence paths, what was tried and the stop rule. A
  full fork (`fork_turns: "all"`) only with `--fork --reason "<why it needs the whole context>"`.
- **Helpers do not spawn.** A helper or a forked agent never starts agents of its own; only the owner
  requests helpers.
- **Record each lane result as you find it:** `python -m ctf spawn result <id> --lane <lane> --spawn
  <spawn id> --status found|ruled_out|partial|blocked --summary "..."`; when you finish, `python -m ctf
  spawn end <spawn id>`. `python -m ctf spawn board` shows the agents on each challenge.

{{brief_discipline}}

## Long-running commands

- **Never poll a command from model turns.** No `sleep` loops, no `ps`/`pgrep` checks, no
  re-tailing a log, no empty `write_stdin` or `wait` polls: each is a paid turn that does nothing.
- A command that may run more than a minute or two (suite, benchmark, crack, solver run):
  `python -m ctf job start --name NAME -- CMD ARGS...` returns at once with a job id and output
  path; then one `python -m ctf job wait ID` blocks with no model turn and returns once, with the
  exit code and the last lines. `python -m ctf job run --name NAME -- CMD` does both. Claude Code:
  run the wait in the background and you are woken when it ends. Codex: run it as one blocking
  command. Exit 124 means `--timeout` passed and the job still runs: wait again. Also `job status`,
  `job tail ID`, `job kill ID` (stops the job and everything it started).

## Waiting on other agents

- **Never poll a child with timed waits.** No short `wait_agent` timeouts, no `list_agents` or
  status `send_message` rounds: each is a paid turn that does nothing.
- A child you start gets an id; its brief ends with
  `python -m ctf coord done <id> --status done|failed|blocked --summary "<result or question>"`
  (add `--path <file>` for result files); a `spawn request` brief already ends with it, and the
  child id is the spawn id. Then one `python -m ctf coord wait <id>...` (`--any`
  for the first) blocks with no model turn and returns once, with each result. It also takes
  `campaign:<agent>`, `job:<id>`, `standby:<agent>`, `claim:<id>` and `msg:<lane>`; wait on a lane
  as `msg:<lane> --as <you>`, so a finding that came in since your last inbox read still wakes you.
  Claude Code: run it in the background with `--no-timeout` (one notification when it ends). Codex:
  one blocking command. Exit 124: wait again.
- Lane state (claims, owners, agents, results) is `python -m ctf coord lanes`, not `list_agents`.
- Send only a new finding or decision:
  `python -m ctf coord send <lane> finding|decision "<text>" --from <you>`; anything else is
  dropped and each lane is capped. Read with `python -m ctf coord inbox <lane> --as <you>`.
  Status goes to `python -m ctf orch report`.

